1
00:00:09,290 --> 00:00:12,480
Let's jump into multiplexing.

2
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:17,420
This is probably the
core requirement

3
00:00:17,420 --> 00:00:21,460
for the transport layer
to be able to handle.

4
00:00:21,790 --> 00:00:26,595
We can be revisiting this
again, Ethernet addressing,

5
00:00:26,595 --> 00:00:29,325
each network card
has a Mac address

6
00:00:29,325 --> 00:00:32,910
and when you send a frame
through the network,

7
00:00:32,910 --> 00:00:34,850
the frame contains the source

8
00:00:34,850 --> 00:00:37,115
and destination Mac addresses.

9
00:00:37,115 --> 00:00:41,150
This is used by the
receiver to say yes,

10
00:00:41,150 --> 00:00:42,610
it's destined for me,

11
00:00:42,610 --> 00:00:45,190
and used by network devices

12
00:00:45,190 --> 00:00:48,815
to be able to forward it
to the particular host.

13
00:00:48,815 --> 00:00:54,670
We saw then that that
really doesn't scale well.

14
00:00:54,670 --> 00:00:57,370
This is where we introduced IP

15
00:00:57,370 --> 00:01:01,030
addressing or for the
Internet Protocol which added

16
00:01:01,030 --> 00:01:03,160
structure to addresses so that

17
00:01:03,160 --> 00:01:06,490
devices could be
grouped and you can

18
00:01:06,490 --> 00:01:09,270
make it so that you
can reach beyond

19
00:01:09,270 --> 00:01:13,495
a local network but
to a global network.

20
00:01:13,495 --> 00:01:17,090
Then we saw in

21
00:01:17,090 --> 00:01:20,110
the introduction that
the first problem is,

22
00:01:20,110 --> 00:01:23,150
well, both of those

23
00:01:23,150 --> 00:01:26,150
addressing schemes
can get me to a host,

24
00:01:26,150 --> 00:01:30,300
but will likely have multiple
applications running.

25
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:33,890
Which application
is the packet for?

26
00:01:34,650 --> 00:01:39,090
This is where the
transport layer comes in,

27
00:01:39,090 --> 00:01:42,275
the networking layer,
and the thonic layer

28
00:01:42,275 --> 00:01:45,890
is for communication
between hosts.

29
00:01:45,890 --> 00:01:49,830
The transport layer is really
about communication between

30
00:01:49,830 --> 00:01:55,980
processes or applications
running on a host.

31
00:01:57,250 --> 00:02:00,350
How is this solved?
You guessed it.

32
00:02:00,350 --> 00:02:06,830
Another header shown
down in the bottom left.

33
00:02:06,830 --> 00:02:10,330
We can see we put a
Ethernet header on it.

34
00:02:10,330 --> 00:02:12,590
We put the IP header on it.

35
00:02:12,590 --> 00:02:14,590
Well, we're going to
introduce a new header

36
00:02:14,590 --> 00:02:16,770
for the transport layer,

37
00:02:17,050 --> 00:02:19,590
Much as each of those had

38
00:02:19,590 --> 00:02:21,905
a source and
destination address.

39
00:02:21,905 --> 00:02:25,250
These are going to
the transport layer

40
00:02:25,250 --> 00:02:29,415
also effectively a source
and destination address.

41
00:02:29,415 --> 00:02:31,910
The addresses here are called

42
00:02:31,910 --> 00:02:38,030
ports inside of your computer,

43
00:02:38,030 --> 00:02:40,950
inside the host the
operating system.

44
00:02:40,950 --> 00:02:44,230
As a notion of ports for

45
00:02:44,230 --> 00:02:48,410
different communication paths,
different applications,

46
00:02:48,410 --> 00:02:53,930
we would commonly run a
web server on port 80,

47
00:02:53,930 --> 00:02:58,410
an SSH server would
be run on port 22.

48
00:02:58,410 --> 00:03:02,070
Those ports are what's
relating to the fields,

49
00:03:02,070 --> 00:03:05,745
the source and
destination port numbers

50
00:03:05,745 --> 00:03:08,500
in the transport header.

51
00:03:08,780 --> 00:03:13,790
Now, there's two main protocols

52
00:03:13,790 --> 00:03:19,600
that are commonly
used, UDP and TCP.

53
00:03:19,600 --> 00:03:23,280
Let's go over those briefly.

54
00:03:23,540 --> 00:03:27,625
Now, taking a step aside,

55
00:03:27,625 --> 00:03:30,710
let's talk about message naming.

56
00:03:30,710 --> 00:03:34,630
We've used a lot of
different names.

57
00:03:35,430 --> 00:03:38,615
There are a lot of
different names for

58
00:03:38,615 --> 00:03:43,930
the contents of what
is a message called.

59
00:03:43,930 --> 00:03:47,310
At the link layer,
we call it a frame.

60
00:03:47,310 --> 00:03:49,175
At the network layer,

61
00:03:49,175 --> 00:03:50,815
we call it a packet.

62
00:03:50,815 --> 00:03:52,875
At the transport layer,

63
00:03:52,875 --> 00:03:54,470
we call it a datagram.

64
00:03:54,470 --> 00:03:57,280
That's where we're going to
be calling it this time.

65
00:03:57,280 --> 00:03:59,520
Then at the application layer,

66
00:03:59,520 --> 00:04:01,960
which we'll talk about
in the next module,

67
00:04:01,960 --> 00:04:03,960
we'll call it a message.

68
00:04:03,960 --> 00:04:06,100
Now, a lot of times you can

69
00:04:06,100 --> 00:04:09,680
use these casually
interchangeably,

70
00:04:09,680 --> 00:04:12,780
but if you want to be accurate,

71
00:04:12,780 --> 00:04:17,750
these are the commonly
accepted namings for these.

72
00:04:20,550 --> 00:04:26,360
A distinction that you'll
see in transport protocols

73
00:04:26,360 --> 00:04:29,240
is the distinction
between whether it's

74
00:04:29,240 --> 00:04:32,760
connection less or
connection-oriented.

75
00:04:32,760 --> 00:04:36,330
If it's connection
less, the host,

76
00:04:36,330 --> 00:04:38,590
the operating system
within the host

77
00:04:38,590 --> 00:04:41,590
uses just the destination
IP address and

78
00:04:41,590 --> 00:04:43,570
the destination port to

79
00:04:43,570 --> 00:04:47,010
demultiplex it to the
specific process.

80
00:04:47,010 --> 00:04:51,100
This doesn't require
any pre-establishment

81
00:04:51,100 --> 00:04:53,865
or establishment process.

82
00:04:53,865 --> 00:04:56,430
There's no state that's

83
00:04:56,430 --> 00:04:59,170
really needed on
the host other than

84
00:04:59,170 --> 00:05:06,110
this tuple of IP port
to process mapping.

85
00:05:06,110 --> 00:05:10,650
That's connection less
connection-oriented,

86
00:05:10,650 --> 00:05:13,545
does have state,

87
00:05:13,545 --> 00:05:18,275
so a four tuple of the
source IP destination,

88
00:05:18,275 --> 00:05:21,710
IP addresses and the
source and destination

89
00:05:21,710 --> 00:05:26,110
ports identifies a
unique connection.

90
00:05:26,110 --> 00:05:29,470
You can have
multiple connections

91
00:05:29,470 --> 00:05:33,790
to being handled by
a single process.

92
00:05:33,790 --> 00:05:37,050
That's where all this
extra information

93
00:05:37,050 --> 00:05:40,540
is needed because
that represents that.

94
00:05:40,540 --> 00:05:42,580
Now, this requires

95
00:05:42,580 --> 00:05:48,180
establishment before the
actual data transfer.

96
00:05:48,180 --> 00:05:50,250
The host, as we'll see,

97
00:05:50,250 --> 00:05:54,270
keeps a lot more state
about the connection.

98
00:05:55,270 --> 00:05:59,610
UDP, the first main

99
00:05:59,610 --> 00:06:03,370
protocol in the transport
layer is connection less.

100
00:06:03,370 --> 00:06:05,085
This is real simple.

101
00:06:05,085 --> 00:06:07,590
The main thing it provides is

102
00:06:07,590 --> 00:06:10,250
the multiplexing
and demultiplexing,

103
00:06:10,250 --> 00:06:14,690
then it just inherits
IP's service model.

104
00:06:14,690 --> 00:06:18,250
The best effort delivery
of each datagram.

105
00:06:18,250 --> 00:06:22,270
You could see the header
for UDP is quite simple.

106
00:06:22,270 --> 00:06:24,530
It has the source
and destination

107
00:06:24,530 --> 00:06:27,650
ports and then a length field to

108
00:06:27,650 --> 00:06:30,030
be able to say know

109
00:06:30,030 --> 00:06:34,830
how much data is
inside of the payload.

110
00:06:35,510 --> 00:06:41,830
TCP, on the other hand,
is connection-oriented.

111
00:06:42,320 --> 00:06:44,770
As I mentioned, a single process

112
00:06:44,770 --> 00:06:47,390
can have multiple connections.

113
00:06:47,390 --> 00:06:49,930
This four tuple of

114
00:06:49,930 --> 00:06:52,250
the source destination
IP addresses and

115
00:06:52,250 --> 00:06:54,910
the source destination
port numbers

116
00:06:54,910 --> 00:06:57,690
identifies that connection.

117
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:02,950
A lot of times you'll hear
this called a five tuple,

118
00:07:02,950 --> 00:07:06,910
which includes the
protocol type as well,

119
00:07:06,910 --> 00:07:09,710
whether it's TCP or UDP.

120
00:07:09,710 --> 00:07:12,230
In this case, it would
be the five tuple

121
00:07:12,230 --> 00:07:14,220
of TCP as the protocol.

122
00:07:14,220 --> 00:07:17,780
Then those four addresses.

123
00:07:17,780 --> 00:07:21,470
The service model
for TCP is that it

124
00:07:21,470 --> 00:07:25,290
provides an in order
reliable stream.

125
00:07:25,290 --> 00:07:28,290
We'll get into the details of

126
00:07:28,290 --> 00:07:31,530
how TCP provides this
reliable stream,

127
00:07:31,530 --> 00:07:33,830
but all we needed

128
00:07:33,830 --> 00:07:36,590
to introduce in this
video was the concept

129
00:07:36,590 --> 00:07:42,830
of multiplexing.
Until next time.